<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishikawa Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ishikawa Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region">Hokuriku region</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>. It covers the old provinces of Kaga and Noto, which formed the core of the Kaga domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda clan</a>, the richest domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The prefecture was created in 1872 after the abolition of the domains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishikawa-prefecture.jpg" alt="Ishikawa Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Winniepix · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenrokuen_Garden,_Kanazawa_(53621201294).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ishikawa lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, in the historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region">Hokuriku region</a>. It covers about 4,191 square kilometres and borders Toyama Prefecture to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu">Gifu</a> to the south-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> to the south. Its northern part is the narrow Noto Peninsula, which forms Toyama Bay, one of the largest bays in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The southern part is wider and mostly mountainous, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>, the capital and largest city, stands on the coastal plain. Other major cities are Hakusan, Komatsu and Kaga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ishikawa was formed in 1872 from the merger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaga-province">Kaga Province</a> and the smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noto-province">Noto Province</a>, with its government at first at Mikawa. The seat moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> in 1873. In 1878 six shizoku, former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> dissatisfied with the Meiji government, assassinated the statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Ōkubo Toshimichi</a> in the Kioizaka Incident. The national government feared that a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region">Hokuriku</a> bloc might form in support of the Freedom and People's Rights Movement and wished to weaken the former Kaga lords, so it divided the prefecture in two stages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui-prefecture">Fukui Prefecture</a> was formed in 1881 and Toyama Prefecture in 1883.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ishikawa was formerly an important populated centre that held some of the wealthiest han of the feudal period. Noh was introduced under the fifth Maeda lord, Tsunanori, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> came in 1666 when Maeda Toshitsune invited Senbiki Soshitsu of Urasenke to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>. Kutani ware, Kaga silk, Kanazawa lacquerware decorated with gold dust and Kanazawa gold leaf are among its best-known products. Kanazawa Castle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenroku-en">Kenroku-en</a>, one of the Three Great Gardens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, stand in the capital. Industry is dominated by textiles, particularly artificial fabrics, and by machinery, particularly construction machinery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ishikawa Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A magnitude 7.5 earthquake struck <b>Ishikawa Prefecture</b> on New Year's Day, triggering tsunami warnings, fires and landslides.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was abolished in 1871, becoming the core of <b>Ishikawa Prefecture</b>, and Kanazawa remains a cultural centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaga-domain">Kaga Domain</a></li></ul></section>
